{"id":891,"date":"2020-11-10T22:40:27","date_gmt":"2020-11-11T06:40:27","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=891"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:30","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:30","slug":"overview-of-rokit-smart","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/overview-of-rokit-smart\/","title":{"rendered":"Overview of Rokit Smart"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215df46bc7\" name=\"6a2215df46bc7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What's inside a Rokit Smart kit?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Every Rokit Smart kit will include the following pieces to make 11 awesome robots:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 main board<\/li>\n<li>2 DC motors (360\u00ba rotation)<\/li>\n<li>2 horn adapters<\/li>\n<li>1 carrying kit complete with nuts, bolts, and screws<\/li>\n<li>4 blue peg boards<\/li>\n<li>1 micro USB cable<\/li>\n<li>1 USB to UART connector cable<\/li>\n<li>Assorted frame set<\/li>\n<li>1 battery box<\/li>\n<li>1 remote control with a battery<\/li>\n<li>1 Phillips screw driver<\/li>\n<li>1 nut driver<\/li>\n<li>2 wheels<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The USB to UART connector cable is used to connect the Smart Inventor Board to your computer so you can upload your Arduino code onto your Rokit Smart robot.<\/p>\n<p>Make sure you know the different between a nut (not the kind you eat), a bolt, and a screw!<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A nut is used to secure either a bolt or screw.<\/li>\n<li>A screw has a sharp point.<\/li>\n<li>A bolt has a flat end.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;892&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215df46ce4\" name=\"6a2215df46ce4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Smart Inventor Board<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The Smart Inventor Board has the following components built in to help your robots work:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>4 DC motor pins<\/li>\n<li>5 external digital ports<\/li>\n<li>5 external analog ports<\/li>\n<li>1 buzzer<\/li>\n<li>3 DIP switches<\/li>\n<li>8 blue LED lights<\/li>\n<li>3 IR sensors on the top<\/li>\n<li>7 IR sensors on the bottom<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Don&#8217;t worry if you don&#8217;t know what any of these are yet! We&#8217;ll be going over how to use all of these components in almost every lesson.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215df46d60\" name=\"6a2215df46d60\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Choosing the right program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your board comes pre-installed with 11 programs for our 11 default robot builds. To choose the programs, turn your Smart Inventor Board on by flipping the power switch to the &#8220;ON&#8221; position. By default, the board has a LED positioned for the first program. To choose a program, use the left and right IR sensors.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The left IR sensor makes you move down a program (you will see the LED position move to the left).<\/li>\n<li>The right IR sensor makes you move up a program (you will see the LED position move to the right).<\/li>\n<li>The center IR sensor lets you choose the program you&#8217;re on.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;897&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;898&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215df46dc4\" name=\"6a2215df46dc4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Uploading your own code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you want to use and upload your own code to the Smart Inventor Board, follow these steps:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Place DIP switch 1 in the \u201cON\u201d position on the Smart Inventor Board, as shown below.<\/li>\n<li>Connect the USB to UART connector to the Micro USB cable and attach it to the board.<\/li>\n<li>The far-right LED will start blinking, which lets you know that you are in programming mode.<\/li>\n<li>While in programming mode, you can create your own programs in the Arduino IDE or even modify our robot programs (which you can find in the robot tutorials).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Note: you need to install the Arduino IDE to do this![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;899&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 5 What's inside a Rokit Smart kit? [vc_column_text]Every Rokit Smart kit will include the following pieces to make 11 awesome robots: 1 main board 2 DC motors (360\u00ba&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/overview-of-rokit-smart\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1753,1617,626,1533,1438,144,171],"lesson-tag":[2163,2174,2173,2167,2122,2172,2171,2155],"class_list":["post-891","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-coding","lesson-tag-electronics","lesson-tag-overview","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-robotics","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","lesson-tag-sensor","module-getting-started","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"